From 05a25daaf1681f89db64f0e84d16c556bafd8463 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wray-lee <wray-lee@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 01:39:42 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] feat(telegram): opt-in rich messages via
 sendRichMessage

Add a per-account `channels.telegram.accounts.<id>.rich_messages` flag
(default false). When enabled, single-message replies (<= 32768 Unicode
codepoints) and in-place edits are sent through the Bot API rich-message
path (sendRichMessage / editMessageText rich_message) with GFM Markdown,
so headings, tables, task lists, footnotes, ==mark==, ||spoiler|| and
$math$ render natively.

- config_types: TelegramConfig.rich_messages (default false)
- telegram: rich path is used only when rich_messages is enabled and the
  escaped reply fits in a single rich message (<= 32768 codepoints,
  measured after escaping); longer messages, or any rich send/edit error,
  fall back directly to the legacy split/HTML path; non-rich Telegram errors
  (e.g. 401, 403, 429, chat not found) are not retried twice
- telegram: escapeRichMarkdown passes the model's own GFM through,
  escaping only `<` outside code spans, fenced blocks, and indented code,
  and degrades GFM tables wider than 20 columns to a code block
- telegram_api: sendRichMessage / editMessageRich client methods,
  buildSendRichMessageBody / buildEditMessageRichBody, and
  responseIsRichRelatedError
- prompt: tell the model rich Markdown is available only when enabled
- docs: en/zh configuration notes

Off by default because some Desktop/Web/Android and third-party clients
render rich messages as an unsupported placeholder. The rich-off path is
byte-identical to upstream (no changes to shared splitter/limits).

Caveat: the Bot API documents exactly one rich-Markdown escape
(`\#hashtag`); `\<` is assumed from GFM/CommonMark, where any ASCII
punctuation may be backslash-escaped. See escapeRichMarkdown.
